{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00974 - MONDO_1567",
  "verbalization": "Edetic Acid is implicated in the pathogenesis of Nephrocalcinosis through its association with Hypercalcemia Disease, which is a key driver of the condition. The evidence suggests that Edetic Acid may act as a mediator in the development of Nephrocalcinosis by promoting hypercalcemia, which in turn leads to the formation of calcium deposits in the kidneys. This mechanism is supported by the shared indication of Hypercalcemia Disease across multiple pathways, linking Edetic Acid to calcium salts such as Calcium Lactate Gluconate and Calcium Carbonate, all of which are known to contribute to nephrocalcinosis. The consistent pathway from Edetic Acid to Nephrocalcinosis highlights a potential role in calcium metabolism and kidney stone formation. The strength of the evidence is reinforced by the high scores in all three paths, indicating a robust and reliable connection through the common thread of hypercalcemia.",
